 "edits": [
  {
   "op": "replace_brief",
   "brief": "growing-pains",
   "html": "<div class=\"brief sp\" id=\"growing-pains\" data-entry=\"hub\" data-lens=\"peds\" data-spine=\"2\" data-shelf=\"fm peds\" data-bp=\"msk\" data-replaces=\"bs-acute-myositis\">\n<span class=\"alias\" id=\"bs-acute-myositis\"></span>\n<h4>Leg Pain in a Child</h4>\n<p class=\"sub\">growing pains &middot; myositis after influenza &middot; reflexes and Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome &middot; leukemia and bone tumor warning signs &middot; when to radiograph &middot; treatment by cause</p>\n<nav class=\"sp-step\" aria-label=\"Decision spine\"><a href=\"#lg-s1\"><b>1</b><span>Presentation</span></a><a href=\"#lg-s2\"><b>2</b><span>Urgent</span></a><a href=\"#lg-dx\"><b>3</b><span>Differential</span></a><a href=\"#lg-s4\"><b>4</b><span>Workup</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>5</b><span>Criteria</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>6</b><span>Severity</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>7</b><span>Treatment</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>8</b><span>Follow-up</span></a><a href=\"#blp-bank\" class=\"sp-prac\"><span>Practice</span></a></nav>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"lg-s1\"><b class=\"sp-n\">1</b>Presentation</h5>\n<div class=\"dp\"><span class=\"lbl\">Bottom line</span><ul class=\"bl\"><li><b>Both legs</b>, pain in the <b>evening or night</b> with <b>pain-free days</b>, and a <b>normal exam</b>: <b>growing pains</b>; <b>reassure</b>, with <b>no labs or radiographs</b></li><li><b>Tender calves</b> a few days into <b>influenza</b>, <b>reflexes kept</b>: <b>benign acute childhood myositis</b>; <b>CK</b> (creatine kinase) is high, and <b>dark urine</b> points to <b>rhabdomyolysis</b></li><li><b>Ascending weakness</b> with <b>absent reflexes</b>: <b>Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome</b>. <b>Bone tenderness</b> with <b>pallor or bruising</b>: <b>CBC with smear</b>. <b>One side</b> or a <b>focal spot</b>: <b>radiograph</b></li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"sp-script sp-s2\"><div class=\"sr\"><span class=\"sk\">What it is</span><div class=\"sv\"><p>Pain in the legs of a child, sorted by the exam</p></div></div><div class=\"sr\"><span class=\"sk\">Sort by</span><div class=\"sv\"><p>The exam first: gait, joints, bones, calves, reflexes and skin, then the side, the timing and any systemic signs <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"lg-pop-3\">Night pain</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"lg-pop-3\" hidden><span class=\"sp-ct\">Shared by benign and serious causes</span>Growing pains, osteoid osteoma and leukemia can all hurt at night, and a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) often eases both growing pains and osteoid osteoma; the side, the exam and systemic signs decide</span></p></div></div><div class=\"sr\"><span class=\"sk\">Hands off</span><div class=\"sv\"><p>Bone tumors, leukemia, the hot limb and dark urine have their own briefs<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bone-tumors\">Bone Lesions in a Child</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-leukemia\">Acute Leukemia in a Child</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"limp\">The Limping Child</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"dipstick-mismatch\">Dark Urine: Hemoglobin, Myoglobin or Blood</button></div></p></div></div></div>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"lg-s2\"><b class=\"sp-n\">2</b>Urgent risks</h5>\n<div class=\"sp-rows\">\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Respiratory failure</span><b class=\"sp-f\">Ascending weakness with absent reflexes: Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome can paralyze the breathing muscles</b><p class=\"sp-commit\"><b>If</b> the weakness rises and the reflexes are gone, <b>choose</b> <span class=\"mask\">admit; serial spirometry, intubating when the forced vital capacity falls to 20 mL/kg or less (the threshold taught for the exam)</span>. <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"lg-pop-2\">In practice</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"lg-pop-2\" hidden><span class=\"sp-ct\">Vital capacity</span>a standard clinical reference sets a lower threshold: intubation when the vital capacity falls below 15 mL/kg, checked every 6 to 8 hours</span></p></div>\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Rhabdomyolysis</span><b class=\"sp-f\">Tea-colored urine, a dipstick positive for blood with few red cells, and a CK above 1,000</b><div class=\"sp-chips\"><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"dipstick-mismatch\">Dark Urine: Hemoglobin, Myoglobin or Blood</button></div></div><p class=\"sp-commit\"><b>If</b> the dipstick shows blood with few red cells, <b>choose</b> <span class=\"mask\">IV fluids; follow creatinine and potassium</span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Leukemia</span><b class=\"sp-f\">Bone pain with pallor, bruising or fever; the pain can be worse at night and in both legs</b><div class=\"sp-chips\"><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-leukemia\">Acute Leukemia in a Child</button></div></div><p class=\"sp-commit\"><b>If</b> the bones are tender with pallor or bruising, <b>choose</b> <span class=\"mask\">CBC with peripheral smear</span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Hot limb</span><b class=\"sp-f\">Fever with one-sided point tenderness, warmth or refusal to bear weight: osteomyelitis or septic arthritis</b><div class=\"sp-chips\"><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"limp\">The Limping Child</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"septic-hip\">Septic Arthritis and Transient Synovitis in a Child</button></div></div><p class=\"sp-commit\"><b>If</b> one leg is tender and the child is febrile, <b>choose</b> <span class=\"mask\">ESR (erythrocyte sedimentation rate), CRP (C-reactive protein) and urgent imaging</span>.</p></div>\n</div>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"lg-dx\"><b class=\"sp-n\">3</b>Differential</h5>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Leg pain in a child, most tempting first</caption><thead><tr><th>Diagnosis</th><th>Fingerprint</th><th>Decides it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Growing pains <span class=\"aka\">(benign nocturnal limb pains of childhood)</span> <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"lg-pop-4\">How common</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"lg-pop-4\" hidden><span class=\"sp-ct\">Growing pains</span>Between 80% and 90% describe both legs; episodes last minutes to hours</span></td><td><span>Onset usually 3 to 6 years; calves, thighs, shins or behind the knee, often after active days; late in the day or at night, not during play</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Months, with pain-free days</b>: both legs, evening or night, gone by morning; normal exam and gait; no systemic signs</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Benign acute childhood myositis <span class=\"aka\">(influenza-induced myositis)</span></td><td><span>School-age, more often boys; about 3 days after a flu-like illness, most often influenza B; sudden calf pain, difficulty or refusal to walk</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Days</b>: calves tender to touch; reflexes preserved; CK high, CRP usually normal <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"lg-pop-1\">Weakness</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"lg-pop-1\" hidden><span class=\"sp-ct\">Myositis and Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome</span>Influenza myositis can weaken a child, from mild weakness to quadriparesis, so strength does not separate it from Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome; preserved reflexes do</span></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome</td><td><span>After an infection, influenza among them; symmetric weakness that usually starts in the legs and rises</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Days to weeks</b>: deep tendon reflexes lost; no calf tenderness; CSF (cerebrospinal fluid) protein may be high with a normal cell count</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-leukemia\">Acute Leukemia in a Child</button></div></td><td><span>Dull bone pain, worse at night; refuses to walk; fever, pallor, bruising</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Tender long bones with pallor or bruising; cytopenias and blasts on the CBC and smear</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Bone tumor (osteoid osteoma, osteosarcoma)<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bone-tumors\">Bone Lesions in a Child</button></div></td><td><span>One side; persistent or progressive pain; osteoid osteoma hurts at night and NSAIDs relieve it</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Radiograph: a small lucent nidus (osteoid osteoma), or a destructive lesion with periosteal reaction and a soft-tissue mass (osteosarcoma)</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Juvenile dermatomyositis</td><td><span>Proximal weakness: trouble climbing stairs or rising from a seat</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Weeks to months</b>: heliotrope rash on the eyelids, Gottron papules on the knuckles; CK high</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Arthritis (juvenile idiopathic arthritis, parvovirus B19, rheumatic fever)<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"sjia\">Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-fever-rash-arthralgia\">Fever and Rash in a Child</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"arf\">Acute Rheumatic Fever</button></div></td><td><span>Pain in the joint, worse in the morning</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Joint swelling or reduced range of motion; morning stiffness</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Overuse injury (Osgood-Schlatter disease)<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"limp\">The Limping Child</button></div></td><td><span>Active child after the growth spurt; pain with activity</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Tender tibial tubercle; pain on resisted knee extension</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"lcw\"><div class=\"ttl\"><span>Time course</span><small>not to scale</small></div><ol class=\"wrows\"><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Benign acute childhood myositis</b><span class=\"win\">About 3 days after the illness begins; self-limited</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:22%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:45%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:68%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c1\" style=\"--a:0%;--b:22%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Illness</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:22%\">1 week</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:45%\">1 month</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:68%\">3 months</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">Years</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome</b><span class=\"win\">Days to weeks; weakness peaks by 3 to 4 weeks in 90%</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:22%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:45%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:68%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar inf\" style=\"--a:8%;--b:45%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Illness</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:22%\">1 week</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:45%\">1 month</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:68%\">3 months</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">Years</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Juvenile dermatomyositis</b><span class=\"win\">Weakness may build over weeks to months</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:22%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:45%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:68%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c3\" style=\"--a:30%;--b:70%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Illness</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:22%\">1 week</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:45%\">1 month</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:68%\">3 months</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">Years</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Growing pains</b><span class=\"win\">Episodes for months to years, with pain-free days; gone by adolescence</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:22%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:45%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:68%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c3\" style=\"--a:55%;--b:100%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Illness</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:22%\">1 week</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:45%\">1 month</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:68%\">3 months</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">Years</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Bone tumor</b><span class=\"win\">One side; persistent or progressive</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:22%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:45%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:68%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar inf\" style=\"--a:45%;--b:100%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Illness</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:22%\">1 week</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:45%\">1 month</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:68%\">3 months</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">Years</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li></ol><div class=\"lc-key\"><span><i class=\"k-ac\"></i>Days</span><span><i class=\"k-chr\"></i>Weeks or longer</span><span><i class=\"k-inf\"></i>Do not miss</span></div></figure>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"lg-s4\"><b class=\"sp-n\">4</b>Workup</h5>\n<figure class=\"lcp w5a\"><p class=\"kick\">Diagnostic workup</p><p class=\"w5z\">First step</p><div class=\"w5t first\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Every child with leg pain</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">History: side, timing, activity, a recent illness, systemic symptoms</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Both legs, evening or night, pain-free days</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Growing pains pattern</span></div><div class=\"w5fd\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">A few days after a flu-like illness</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m\"><span class=\"mask\">Myositis or Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Fever, fatigue, weight loss, pallor or bruising</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Systemic cause: not growing pains</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b w5k\"><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Exam: gait, joints, palpation of bones and calves, strength and reflexes, skin</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Normal exam and gait</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Growing pains: no tests</span></div><div class=\"w5fd\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Tender calves, reflexes kept</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m\"><span class=\"mask\">Myositis: CK and urinalysis</span></div><div class=\"w5fd\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Weakness rising from the legs, reflexes absent</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m\"><span class=\"mask\">Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome: see Next step</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Bone tenderness, a focal spot, a mass or joint swelling</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Radiograph or laboratory tests</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5z\">Targeted tests</p><div class=\"w5t\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Tender calves after influenza</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Serum CK</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">CK elevated</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Muscle disease, not a joint or nerve</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b w5k\"><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Urinalysis with microscopy</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Dipstick positive for blood, few red cells, CK above 1,000</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Myoglobin: rhabdomyolysis; IV fluids</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Urinalysis normal</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Home: rest, analgesics and fluids</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Bone tenderness with pallor, bruising or fever</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">CBC with peripheral smear</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Cytopenias, blasts</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Leukemia<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-leukemia\">Acute Leukemia in a Child</button></div></span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">One side, a focal tender spot, progressive pain or a mass</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Plain radiograph</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Small lucent nidus</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Osteoid osteoma<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bone-tumors\">Bone Lesions in a Child</button></div></span></div><div class=\"w5fd\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Destructive lesion, periosteal reaction, soft-tissue mass</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m\"><span class=\"mask\">Osteosarcoma<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bone-tumors\">Bone Lesions in a Child</button></div></span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Normal film, focal signs persist</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">MRI or bone scan</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5z\">Next step</p><div class=\"w5t\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Ascending weakness, absent reflexes</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Admit; CSF analysis, nerve conduction studies; serial spirometry</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">CSF protein may be high, cell count normal</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Supports Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome; it may take up to a week to appear</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Weeks of proximal weakness with a rash</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">CK; muscle biopsy is the definitive test</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Heliotrope rash, Gottron papules, CK high</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Juvenile dermatomyositis</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5skip\"><span class=\"w5z\">Skip</span><span class=\"mask\">Blood tests and radiographs for a classic history with a normal exam, and new tests when growing pains recur with a normal exam: episodes with pain-free weeks are expected until adolescence.</span></p></figure>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Treatment by cause</caption><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Growing pains</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Explain the benign, self-limited course; massage, sometimes with heat; ibuprofen or acetaminophen; stretching may help; keep usual activity, no braces <span class=\"unv\" title=\"unverified: no source opened 2026-10-21 states it\">&#x26A0;&#xFE0E;</span></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Benign acute childhood myositis</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Rest, analgesics and fluids; avoid aspirin in children with influenza (Reye syndrome); use a non-aspirin analgesic such as ibuprofen or acetaminophen instead<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-reye\">Encephalopathy and Cerebral Edema in a Child</button></div></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Rhabdomyolysis</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">IV fluids to prevent acute kidney injury; follow creatinine and potassium; admit <span class=\"unv\" title=\"unverified: no source opened 2026-10-21 states it\">&#x26A0;&#xFE0E;</span><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"dipstick-mismatch\">Dark Urine: Hemoglobin, Myoglobin or Blood</button></div></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">IVIG (intravenous immune globulin) or plasma exchange, with respiratory support; no glucocorticoids</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Juvenile dermatomyositis</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Glucocorticoids, then long-term methotrexate</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Clues that change the answer</caption><thead><tr><th>Clue</th><th>Points to</th><th>Next move</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Night pain in one leg that ibuprofen relieves</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Osteoid osteoma: relief by NSAIDs does not make it growing pains<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bone-tumors\">Bone Lesions in a Child</button></div></span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Radiograph</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Pain that limits sports or comes during activity</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Not growing pains: an overuse injury or trauma</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Examine the tibial tubercle and the joints</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Calf pain with weakness</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Myositis or Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome: weakness does not separate them</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Test the reflexes: kept in myositis, lost in Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Morning pain and stiffness, joint swelling</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Inflammatory arthritis<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"sjia\">Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis</button></div></span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">ESR, CRP and a CBC</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"sp-pause\"><b>Pause point.</b> The bait: blood tests or films for classic growing pains; reassurance for one-sided night pain because NSAIDs work; and calling rising weakness myositis because the calves hurt. Both legs with a normal exam earn reassurance; one side or a focal finding earns the radiograph; the reflexes separate myositis from Guillain-Barr&eacute; syndrome.</p>\n\n<h5 class=\"authored-hdr\" id=\"blp-bank\">Practice</h5>\n<ol class=\"bank authored\">\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Order plain radiographs\" data-d2=\"Order complete blood count\" data-item-id=\"q_f075f176703357309252\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_e3f3df476da75de495ff\" data-d1-id=\"o_2842922d7488580c90bf\" data-d2-id=\"o_39992248451357b99da2\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\" data-key-why=\"Both legs, night pain, pain-free days, normal exam\" data-d1-why=\"For one side, a focal tender spot or a mass\" data-d2-why=\"For bone tenderness with pallor, bruising or fever\"><span class=\"sl\">6-year-old boy, 3 months of pain in both thighs and shins</span>\n<span class=\"si\">only at night; worse after running days</span>\n<span class=\"si\">pain-free days; not worsening</span>\n<span class=\"si\">massage and ibuprofen help</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Walks, runs and plays normally</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; no weight loss &middot; hip and knee exam normal</span> &rarr; <b>Observation and reassurance</b> &rarr; both legs, episodic night pain with pain-free days, a normal exam and no systemic signs: growing pains, a clinical diagnosis with no film or blood count</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Observation and reassurance\" data-d2=\"Order complete blood count\" data-item-id=\"q_f3dc1a58d1a45216880e\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_a544160d2b615e8c823a\" data-d1-id=\"o_860ce08b1e4b58d9966a\" data-d2-id=\"o_233c855440505b30859f\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\" data-key-why=\"One-sided night pain needs a film, NSAIDs or not\" data-d1-why=\"Reassurance needs both legs; this is one side\" data-d2-why=\"No pallor, bruising or fever; the pain is focal\"><span class=\"sl\">8-year-old boy with 2 months of right thigh pain at night</span>\n<span class=\"si\">wakes him; ibuprofen relieves it dramatically</span>\n<span class=\"si\">the left leg never hurts</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">No mass or limp</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; exam otherwise normal</span> &rarr; <b>Order plain radiographs</b> &rarr; night pain on one side needs a film even when ibuprofen relieves it: osteoid osteoma also hurts at night and answers to NSAIDs</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Observation and reassurance\" data-d2=\"Order plain radiographs\" data-item-id=\"q_790ff3a627b65e83b421\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_e90f192fa3a55c8ab0b9\" data-d1-id=\"o_9e5cbfc653245c908894\" data-d2-id=\"o_5d264657322b57329188\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\" data-key-why=\"Bone tenderness with pallor, bruises and fever\" data-d1-why=\"Growing pains need a normal exam; bones are tender\" data-d2-why=\"Both legs and systemic signs: the marrow, not one bone\"><span class=\"sl\">7-year-old girl with 3 weeks of leg pain that wakes her at night</span>\n<span class=\"si\">intermittent fevers</span>\n<span class=\"si\">pale, with scattered bruises</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Tender to palpation over both tibias</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">T 38.1 °C &middot; hip and knee motion full</span> &rarr; <b>Order a complete blood count with peripheral smear</b> &rarr; bone tenderness with pallor, bruising and fever is not growing pains, which needs a normal exam: CBC with smear for leukemia</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Restrict running until symptoms resolve\" data-d2=\"Recommend knee braces during activity\" data-item-id=\"q_bcc4863685195098a913\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_a415469853535b2d859c\" data-d1-id=\"o_0d5f1bb2a7e75f519557\" data-d2-id=\"o_82a10491214e55449d31\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\" data-key-why=\"Pain late in the day, not during play; play goes on\" data-d1-why=\"Pain that limits play is not growing pains; nothing to restrict\" data-d2-why=\"A normal exam finds nothing to brace\"><span class=\"sl\">7-year-old boy was diagnosed with growing pains 2 weeks ago</span>\n<span class=\"si\">night leg pain after soccer days</span>\n<span class=\"si\">exam and gait normal</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Parents ask about stopping soccer or buying knee braces</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; no weight loss</span> &rarr; <b>Continue normal activity without restriction</b> &rarr; growing pains do not limit activity (pain that does is not growing pains); keep usual activity, no braces &#x26A0;&#xFE0E;; reassure, and offer massage, heat, analgesics or stretching</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Order a bone scan\" data-d2=\"Refer to pediatric rheumatology for evaluation\" data-item-id=\"q_6140b09fbc6e5920933e\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_6ae31b687dd15861a0b2\" data-d1-id=\"o_d8a6113021665e5bbf61\" data-d2-id=\"o_e1b09ef6a473bae511c7\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\" data-key-why=\"Episodes with pain-free weeks recur until adolescence\" data-d1-why=\"A bone scan is for a focal sign the film misses\" data-d2-why=\"No joint swelling, stiffness or rash to refer\"><span class=\"sl\">9-year-old girl was diagnosed with growing pains 1 year ago</span>\n<span class=\"si\">still has night pain in both shins</span>\n<span class=\"si\">a few nights a month, with pain-free weeks</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Growing normally; plays sports</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; joints and gait normal</span> &rarr; <b>Continued reassurance and supportive care</b> &rarr; growing pains wax and wane for months to years and resolve by adolescence; recurrence with a normal exam is expected, not a reason to escalate</li>\n<li data-type=\"mech\" data-d1=\"Growing pains occur only during sleep cycles\" data-d2=\"Malignant bone pain is never relieved by analgesics\" data-item-id=\"q_2be9b5858d8d5f0a9395\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_220e386f7cfe552dabd8\" data-d1-id=\"o_8ac48fb12cbe5a46b7a4\" data-d2-id=\"o_44ea3e7dabda54878ce2\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following best explains why the night timing does not decide this case?\" data-key-why=\"Both share night pain and NSAID relief; the side decides\" data-d1-why=\"They come in the evening or night, not only in sleep\" data-d2-why=\"Too absolute; the decider is one side, not the drug\"><span class=\"sl\">10-year-old boy with night leg pain</span>\n<span class=\"si\">his parents read that night pain means a tumor</span>\n<span class=\"si\">both shins hurt, worse after sports</span>\n<span class=\"si\">pain-free days; ibuprofen helps</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Exam and gait normal</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; no weight loss</span> &rarr; <b>Osteoid osteoma is also nocturnal and also relieved by NSAIDs, so laterality is the discriminator</b> &rarr; night pain and relief from NSAIDs are shared by growing pains and osteoid osteoma, so check the side, not the hour</li>\n<li data-type=\"test\" data-d1=\"Erythrocyte sedimentation rate\" data-d2=\"Antinuclear antibody\" data-item-id=\"q_0a1e03e56a43beb4d4ea\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_3b2655005820c0ac5423\" data-d1-id=\"o_7e2a742414940b05a654\" data-d2-id=\"o_2ba86a17c20ba911d2d5\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following laboratory studies is most likely to support the diagnosis?\" data-src=\"authored\" data-key-why=\"Tender calves, reflexes kept after influenza: muscle\" data-d1-why=\"Inflammation markers are usually normal in myositis\" data-d2-why=\"No rash, joint swelling or weeks of weakness\"><span class=\"sl\">7-year-old boy was diagnosed with influenza B 3 days ago</span>\n<span class=\"si\">now afebrile; woke refusing to walk</span>\n<span class=\"si\">walks on his toes</span>\n<span class=\"si\">both calves tender</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Strength normal when encouraged</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Knee and ankle reflexes normal &middot; T 37.0 °C</span> &rarr; <b>Serum creatine kinase</b> &rarr; tender calves with reflexes intact days into influenza: benign acute childhood myositis; an elevated CK places the problem in muscle</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Admission for intravenous fluids\" data-d2=\"Oral prednisone and a rheumatology referral\" data-item-id=\"q_eee554b97996ff32f98f\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_b740550d5c2a790e8e1c\" data-d1-id=\"o_f1bc0be022e7d7db4816\" data-d2-id=\"o_7009dec0a4120f7f6d91\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\" data-key-why=\"Normal urinalysis: rest, analgesics and fluids at home\" data-d1-why=\"IV fluids are for dark urine with myoglobin\" data-d2-why=\"Self-limited and viral; no steroid or referral\"><span class=\"sl\">6-year-old boy on day 4 of influenza B, now afebrile</span>\n<span class=\"si\">pain in both calves; walks on tiptoe</span>\n<span class=\"si\">calves tender; strength and reflexes normal</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">CK (creatine kinase) elevated</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">T 37.2 °C &middot; urinalysis normal</span> &rarr; <b>Analgesics, fluids and reassurance</b> &rarr; myositis with a normal urinalysis: analgesia, fluids and rest at home; it is self-limited</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Discharge home on oral ibuprofen with follow-up in 1 week\" data-d2=\"Admission for intravenous methylprednisolone\" data-item-id=\"q_0dad14c5794f68ff48b6\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_a40a2f483599135c0df7\" data-d1-id=\"o_a387bdbb2017b6833927\" data-d2-id=\"o_5f4b3812891d98512a4f\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\" data-key-why=\"Dipstick blood, few red cells, high CK: rhabdomyolysis\" data-d1-why=\"Myoglobin threatens the kidneys: IV fluids, not home care\" data-d2-why=\"IV fluids, not steroids, protect the kidneys\"><span class=\"sl\">8-year-old girl with influenza A and 2 days of severe leg pain</span>\n<span class=\"si\">both calves tender</span>\n<span class=\"si\">tea-colored urine</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Urine dipstick strongly positive for blood</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">T 37.4 °C &middot; 0 to 2 red cells per high-power field &middot; CK markedly elevated</span> &rarr; <b>Admission for intravenous fluids and renal monitoring</b> &rarr; a heme-positive dipstick with almost no red cells and a markedly high CK is myoglobin: rhabdomyolysis; admit for intravenous fluids and follow creatinine and potassium</li>\n<li data-type=\"avoid\" data-d1=\"Ibuprofen\" data-d2=\"Acetaminophen\" data-item-id=\"q_ae7c9f939696360148fe\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_99d7cec3d8aea3e6f0cc\" data-d1-id=\"o_779fc0bfd2d130bc2527\" data-d2-id=\"o_05c74646da941a1b6aba\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following analgesics should be avoided?\" data-src=\"authored\" data-key-why=\"Aspirin with influenza risks Reye syndrome\" data-d1-why=\"Ibuprofen is not aspirin, the drug tied to Reye syndrome\" data-d2-why=\"Acetaminophen is not aspirin, the drug tied to Reye syndrome\"><span class=\"sl\">9-year-old boy with influenza, now afebrile</span>\n<span class=\"si\">pain in both calves for 2 days</span>\n<span class=\"si\">calves tender; strength and reflexes normal</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Parents ask what to give for the pain</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">CK elevated &middot; urinalysis normal</span> &rarr; <b>Aspirin</b> &rarr; aspirin in a child with influenza risks Reye syndrome; give ibuprofen or acetaminophen</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Influenza-induced myositis\" data-d2=\"Transverse myelitis\" data-item-id=\"q_0cd4c5408d7826fced82\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_f13bbfe29bbccc54747a\" data-d1-id=\"o_f980b39bf8aff68cbf73\" data-d2-id=\"o_7125a5cebbffdbfaece9\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Rising weakness with absent reflexes after infection\" data-d1-why=\"Myositis keeps reflexes, and the calves are tender\" data-d2-why=\"Needs a sensory level or bladder dysfunction\"><span class=\"sl\">6-year-old boy with 2 days of leg pain</span>\n<span class=\"si\">upper respiratory infection 10 days ago</span>\n<span class=\"si\">trouble climbing stairs, now spreading upward</span>\n<span class=\"si\">symmetric leg weakness; no calf tenderness</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">No sensory level; bladder normal</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Knee and ankle reflexes absent &middot; T 37.0 °C</span> &rarr; <b>Guillain-Barré syndrome</b> &rarr; ascending symmetric weakness with lost reflexes after an infection is Guillain-Barré syndrome: lumbar puncture and watch breathing; myositis keeps reflexes and hurts to touch</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Influenza-induced myositis\" data-d2=\"Systemic lupus erythematosus\" data-item-id=\"q_605ba04e7d17d27321bf\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_feab0e4f0d5596e99923\" data-d1-id=\"o_6c0956e9b60299bf2d22\" data-d2-id=\"o_bab7de70d336d5bbe865\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Weeks of proximal weakness, heliotrope rash, Gottron papules\" data-d1-why=\"Myositis comes days after influenza, with no rash\" data-d2-why=\"Heliotrope rash and Gottron papules mean dermatomyositis\"><span class=\"sl\">8-year-old girl with 2 months of trouble climbing stairs</span>\n<span class=\"si\">struggles to rise from the floor</span>\n<span class=\"si\">violaceous rash on both upper eyelids</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Scaly red papules over the knuckles</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">CK elevated &middot; T 37.0 °C</span> &rarr; <b>Juvenile dermatomyositis</b> &rarr; weeks of proximal weakness with a heliotrope rash and Gottron papules is juvenile dermatomyositis: systemic glucocorticoids, then long-term methotrexate</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Benign acute childhood myositis\" data-d2=\"Juvenile idiopathic arthritis\" data-item-id=\"q_466ecbed11f7240a758a\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_3ae53b017997388c0b36\" data-d1-id=\"o_7da3b9030e7bb0fb7bdf\" data-d2-id=\"o_48465bde12ebd2842267\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Months of evening pain, pain-free days, normal exam\" data-d1-why=\"Myositis follows influenza with tender calves\" data-d2-why=\"Arthritis swells joints and stiffens in the morning\"><span class=\"sl\">7-year-old girl with 4 months of aching in both calves</span>\n<span class=\"si\">evenings only, worse after active days</span>\n<span class=\"si\">pain-free days; no recent illness</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">No calf tenderness; joints and gait normal</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; no weight loss</span> &rarr; <b>Growing pains</b> &rarr; episodic evening leg pain with pain-free days and a normal exam is growing pains; myositis follows influenza with tender calves, and arthritis swells joints</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"pearls nbq\" data-nbme=\"peds-cms-80d5b8\"><span class=\"lbl\">How NBME framed it: question 1</span><p class=\"nbstem\"><span class=\"sl\">12-year-old boy with 2 days of severe pain in both calves</span>\n<span class=\"si\">a week ago: dry cough, sore throat, headache and fever</span>\n<span class=\"si\">all resolved on ibuprofen after 5 days; no medications now</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Marked tenderness of both calves</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">T 37 °C</span></p><p class=\"nbask\">Most likely diagnosis?</p><span class=\"nbh\">Competing answers</span><ul><li><b>Dermatomyositis</b>: a myositis; <span class=\"mask\">but it brings proximal weakness with a heliotrope rash and Gottron papules; the stem describes no rash and no weakness.</span></li><li><b>Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease)</b>: the fever may have just reached its 5-day threshold; <span class=\"mask\">but it has resolved, with no conjunctivitis, rash, node, hand, foot or lip change. NBME used the formal name.</span></li><li><b>Parvovirus B19-induced arthritis</b>: a post-viral ache; <span class=\"mask\">but it is symmetric arthritis of the small joints of the hands, wrists and feet; this tenderness is in muscle.</span></li><li><b>Rheumatic fever</b>: a sore throat came first; <span class=\"mask\">but it follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis and is diagnosed by the Jones criteria (carditis, arthritis, nodules, erythema marginatum, chorea); calf tenderness is none of them.</span></li></ul><p class=\"two\"><b>Down to two:</b> influenza-induced myositis or dermatomyositis. <span class=\"mask\">Influenza-induced myositis: tender calves a few days after a flu-like illness, with no rash or proximal weakness. The stem never names influenza, and the key names the cause rather than the syndrome.</span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"pearls\"><span class=\"lbl\">Pairs with</span> <i>The Limping Child</i>: the gait is abnormal. <i>Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis</i>: one-sided hip, thigh or knee pain in an adolescent. <i>Acute Leukemia in a Child</i>: the systemic picture. <i>Dark Urine: Hemoglobin, Myoglobin or Blood</i>: heme on the dipstick, no red cells.</div>\n\n<div class=\"rule\"><span class=\"lbl\">Transferable rule</span><p>A symptom shared by benign and serious causes does not separate them; find the feature that does. In leg pain, it is the exam and the side, not the hour.</p></div>\n\n</div>",
